This limits the session to the specified GitHub tools and hides the rest of the GitHub toolkit.  
[Enabling or disabling specific tools](/docs/configuring-sessions#enabling-or-disabling-specific-tools)

Create your own OAuth app and pass its config to the session.  
1. Register an app in the provider’s developer console (Google, GitHub, Slack, etc.) and set the **redirect URI** to the one shown in the Composio dashboard.  
2. In the Composio dashboard create a **custom auth config** for that toolkit and copy its ID (`ac_…`).  
3. When you create or update a session, provide the auth‑config ID in the `authConfigs` map so the session uses your app instead of the managed one.  

```python
from composio import Composio

composio = Composio()
session = composio.create(
    user_id="user_123",
    auth_configs={
        "github": "ac_your_github_config",   # your OAuth app
        # other toolkits keep using managed auth
    },
)
```

```typescript
import { Composio } from '@composio/core';

const composio = new Composio();
const session = await composio.create("user_123", {
  authConfigs: {
    github: "ac_your_github_config",   // your OAuth app
    // other toolkits keep using managed auth
  },
});
```

The consent screen will now show the name and logo of the OAuth app you registered, removing the “Composio wants to access your account” wording.  

For full details see the “Using your own OAuth apps” section of the white‑labeling guide [/docs/white-labeling-authentication#using-your-own-oauth-apps] and the “When to use a custom auth config” and “Create a custom auth config” sections of the managed‑vs‑custom auth guide [/docs/custom-app-vs-managed-app#when-to-use-a-custom-auth-config] and [/docs/custom-app-vs-managed-app#create-a-custom-auth-config].

