The `tools` map keys are toolkit slugs and the value’s `enable` array lists the specific tool names you want to expose. All other GitHub actions are omitted from `session.tools()` and cannot be called by the agent.  

See the **“Enabling or disabling specific tools”** section of the session‑configuration guide for details: [/docs/configuring-sessions#enabling-or-disabling-specific-tools](/docs/configuring-sessions#enabling-or-disabling-specific-tools).

Use the **“Using sessions via MCP”** guide — it walks through exposing a single user’s session through a hosted MCP endpoint.  
Create the session with the `mcp: true` flag (and, if you want a fixed set of tools, combine it with the **direct‑tools preset**).  

```typescript
import { Composio, SessionPreset } from "@composio/core";

const composio = new Composio();
const session = await composio.create("user_123", {
  toolkits: ["gmail"],                     // optional: limit toolkits
  tools: { gmail: { enable: ["GMAIL_FETCH_EMAILS","GMAIL_CREATE_EMAIL_DRAFT"] } },
  sessionPreset: SessionPreset.DIRECT_TOOLS, // optional: fixed‑tool URL
  mcp: true,                               // <‑‑ expose MCP endpoint
});

const mcpUrl = session.mcp.url;
const mcpHeaders = session.mcp.headers;
```

The same pattern works in Python with `composio.sessions.create(..., mcp=True)`.  
See the **MCP endpoint** and **A single URL for a fixed set of tools** sections of the guide for details — [/docs/sessions-via-mcp#the-mcp-endpoint](/docs/sessions-via-mcp#the-mcp-endpoint) and [/docs/sessions-via-mcp#a-single-url-for-a-fixed-set-of-tools](/docs/sessions-via-mcp#a-single-url-for-a-fixed-set-of-tools).

Use the `tools` configuration to whitelist only the GitHub actions you need. Within the `tools` object, give the `github` toolkit an `enable` list (or the shorthand array) containing the desired tool names.

```python
session = composio.sessions.create(
    user_id="user_123",
    tools={
        "github": {
            "enable": ["GITHUB_CREATE_ISSUE", "GITHUB_GET_ISSUE"]
        }
    },
)
```

```typescript
const session = await composio.create("user_123", {
  tools: {
    github: {
      enable: ["GITHUB_CREATE_ISSUE", "GITHUB_GET_ISSUE"],
    },
  },
});
```

Only `GITHUB_CREATE_ISSUE` and `GITHUB_GET_ISSUE` will be exposed; all other GitHub tools are hidden. See the full guide in the **Enabling or disabling specific tools** section of the session config docs [/docs/configuring-sessions#enabling-or-disabling-specific-tools](/docs/configuring-sessions#enabling-or-disabling-specific-tools).

Create a session with the `mcp:true` flag and read the endpoint from `session.mcp`. The URL and required headers are exposed on `session.mcp.url` and `session.mcp.headers`, which you can feed directly into your existing MCP client.  

```python
from composio import Composio

composio = Composio()
session = composio.sessions.create(user_id="my_user", mcp=True)

mcp_url = session.mcp.url
mcp_headers = session.mcp.headers
```

```typescript
import { Composio } from "@composio/core";

const composio = new Composio();
const session = await composio.create("my_user", { mcp: true });

const mcpUrl = session.mcp.url;
const mcpHeaders = session.mcp.headers;
```

Use `mcpUrl` and `mcpHeaders` with your MCP‑compatible client. See the full guide in the MCP docs: [Using sessions via MCP](/docs/sessions-via-mcp#the-mcp-endpoint).

