Then pass `mcpUrl` and `mcpHeaders` to your framework’s MCP client. This follows the recommended guide and configuration for exposing a session over MCP.

Use the **`tools`** configuration when you create the session and list only the GitHub actions you want. The `tools` key maps a toolkit slug to an `enable` (or `disable`) list, so you can expose just a subset of the GitHub toolkit instead of the whole set.

```python
# Python SDK
session = composio.sessions.create(
    user_id="user_123",
    tools={
        "github": {
            "enable": [
                "GITHUB_CREATE_ISSUE",   # only allow creating issues
                "GITHUB_GET_ISSUE",      # and reading issues
            ]
        }
    },
)
```

```typescript
// TypeScript SDK
const session = await composio.create("user_123", {
  tools: {
    // Only these GitHub tools will be available
    github: {
      enable: ["GITHUB_CREATE_ISSUE", "GITHUB_GET_ISSUE"],
    },
  },
});
```

You can also use the shorthand array syntax, which is equivalent to `enable`:

```python
session = composio.sessions.create(
    user_id="user_123",
    tools={"github": ["GITHUB_CREATE_ISSUE", "GITHUB_GET_ISSUE"]},
)
```

```typescript
const session = await composio.create("user_123", {
  tools: { github: ["GITHUB_CREATE_ISSUE", "GITHUB_GET_ISSUE"] },
});
```

These examples are taken from the **Enabling or disabling specific tools** section of the session‑configuration guide [/docs/configuring-sessions#enabling-or-disabling-specific-tools](/docs/configuring-sessions#enabling-or-disabling-specific-tools).
